\fs96 \cf2 Mojo Manual\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Welcome to the Mojo Manual, your complete guide to the Mojo\uc0\u55357 \u56613  programming language!\
Combined with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/lib/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Mojo API reference}}, this documentation provides everything you need to write high-performance Mojo code for CPUs and GPUs. If you see anything that can be improved, please {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/modular/modular/issues/new?assignees=&labels=documentation%2Cmodular-repo%2Cmojo&projects=&template=doc_issue.yaml&title=%5BDocs%5D"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 file an issue}} or send a pull request for the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/modular/modular/tree/modular/v26.2/mojo/docs"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 docs on GitHub}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 About Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo is a systems programming language specifically designed for high-performance AI infrastructure and heterogeneous hardware. Its Pythonic syntax makes it easy for Python programmers to learn and it fully integrates the existing Python ecosystem, including its wealth of AI and machine-learning libraries.\
It's the first programming language built from the ground-up using MLIR\'97a modern compiler infrastructure for heterogeneous hardware, from CPUs to GPUs and other AI ASICs. That means you can use one language to write all your code, from high-level AI applications all the way down to low-level GPU kernels, without using any hardware-specific libraries (such as CUDA and ROCm).\
Learn more about it in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/vision/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Mojo vision}} doc.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Key features\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Python syntax & interop: Mojo adopts (and extends) Python's syntax and integrates with existing Python code. Mojo's interoperability works in both directions, so you can import Python libraries into Mojo and create Mojo bindings to call from Python. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/python/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Read about Python interop}}.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Struct-based types: All data types\'97including basic types such as 
\fs28 \cf11 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf11 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \'97are defined as structs. No types are built into the language itself. That means you can define your own types that have all the the same capabilities as the standard library types. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/structs/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Read about structs}}.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Zero-cost traits: Mojo's trait system solves the problem of static typing by letting you define a shared set of behaviors that types (structs) can implement. It allows you to write functions that depend on traits rather than specific types, similar to interfaces in Java or protocols in Swift, except with compile-time type checking and no run-time performance cost. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Read about traits}}.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Value semantics: Mojo supports both value and reference semantics, but generally defaults to value semantics. With value semantics, each copy is independent\'97modifying one copy won't affect another. With reference semantics, multiple variables can point to the same instance (sometimes called an object), so changes made through one variable are visible through all others. Mojo-native types predominantly use value semantics, which prevents multiple variables from unexpectedly sharing the same data. Read about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/value-semantics/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 value semantics}}.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Value ownership: Mojo's ownership system ensures that only one variable "owns" a specific value at a given time\'97such that Mojo can safely deallocate the value when the owner's lifetime ends\'97while still allowing you to share references to the value. This provides safety from errors such as use-after-free, double-free, and memory leaks without the overhead cost of a garbage collector. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Read about ownership}}.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Compile-time metaprogramming: Mojo's parameterization system enables powerful metaprogramming in which the compiler generates a unique version of a type or function based on parameter values, similar to C++ templates, but more intuitive. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Read about parameterization}}.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Hardware portability: Mojo is designed from the ground up to support heterogeneous hardware\'97the Mojo compiler makes no assumptions about whether your code is written for CPUs, GPUs, or something else. Instead, hardware behaviors are handled by Mojo libraries, as demonstrated by types such as 
\fs28 \cf11 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that allows you to write vectorized code for CPUs, and the 
\fs28 \cf11 \cb12 gpu
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  package that enables hardware-agnostic GPU programming. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/gpu/fundamentals/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Read about GPU programming}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

